WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.790
I remember a couple years ago, you and I were on the phone.

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:12.040
And I said, Ron, you know, what would we say if somebody asked us for the five

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:17.012
things that families should do to really be effective in supporting

00:00:17.012 --> 00:00:18.220
their children&amp;#39;s development?

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:21.920
And by golly, you went off and took that question and ran with it.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.780
So could you talk a little bit about that intervention?

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:25.400
Yeah.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.130
I just want to put it in context a little bit.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:28.838
For the Achievement Gap Initiative, we&amp;#39;re

00:00:28.838 --> 00:00:33.090
trying to do work that hits on the parenting, school, and peer groups,

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.180
as supported by the business community and the larger community.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.490
And so we&amp;#39;re trying to have work going on in each of those circles.

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:42.340
And so what we&amp;#39;re talking about here are the things that

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:43.980
have to do with parenting and families.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.450
And we&amp;#39;ve distilled from the research literature five principles,

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:52.580
you might call them, of early childhood parenting and caregiving.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:56.700
Boston Basics are what we call five fun and simple things

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.390
that any parent can do beginning at birth.

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:07.180
And they are to maximize love and manage stress; to talk, sing, and point;

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:12.470
to count, group, and compare; to explore through movement;

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.830
and to read and discuss.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:18.390
And our intention is to saturate first one neighborhood

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.590
in Boston, then the rest of the city.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:25.570
And after that, we&amp;#39;ll have a kit that people anywhere can use.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:31.930
The nugget for me that affected what our emphasis is with what now we&amp;#39;re

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:37.140
calling the Boston Basics was probably four or five years ago,

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.850
looking at the Early Childhood Longitudinal Survey

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:46.840
and seeing that racial and socioeconomic differences are not

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.560
very apparent around the first birthday, but they&amp;#39;re

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.110
stark by the second birthday.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:59.190
If we know that, then how can we not do something about it?

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:03.820
I mean the gaps we&amp;#39;re interested in are starting to open up so early.

